      "property_id": "PROP-01b-partial1-inv-034",
      "classification": "vulnerability",
      "code_path": "sui-move-contract/contracts/protocol/sources/internal/market/market.move::accrue_interest::L1015-1028",
      "proof_trace": "The function signature of `reserve.accrue_interest` takes a `reserve_factor` parameter and correctly credits `reserve_factor * interest_accumulated` to `cash_reserve` (reserve.move:143). However, the only caller (`market.move:1025`) passes `asset.repay_fee_rate()` \u2014 documented as 'fee rate paid to the protocol in repay' \u2014 in place of a separate reserve_factor; `AssetConfig` has no dedicated reserve_factor field. Additionally, `repay_fee_rate` is never applied during actual loan repayments (`handle_repay` calls `reserve.repay_amount(coin)` with no fee deduction), so no repay fee is charged and interest accrual credits only a tiny fraction (e.g., 1 bp) to the treasury instead of the intended reserve factor.",
      "attack_scenario": "A borrower opens any loan; as time elapses every interaction (borrow, repay, liquidate) triggers `accrue_interest`, which credits `repay_fee_rate * interest` to the protocol treasury rather than the intended `reserve_factor * interest`. With the test-configured rate of 1 basis point versus a typical 1% reserve factor, the protocol treasury receives ~100\u00d7 less than designed, with the shortfall implicitly benefiting suppliers via higher effective supply yield.",
      "checklist_id": "PROP-01b-partial1-inv-034"
    },
    "text": "Reserve factor portion of interest must go to protocol treasury, not to suppliers.",
    "assertion": "reserve += interest_accumulated * reserve_factor && supply_rate = borrow_rate * utilization * (1-reserve_factor)",
    "covers": "SG-010",
    "severity": "MEDIUM",
    "type": "invariant"
  }
}

